            "result_text": "Done! I've created `/app/run.py` with the `run_tasks` function that:\n\n1. **Limits concurrency** using `asyncio.Semaphore` to ensure at most `max_concurrent` tasks run at once\n2. **Handles cleanup on interruption** — when you hit Ctrl+C, asyncio cancels pending tasks, which triggers their `try/finally` cleanup blocks\n3. **Uses standard async patterns** — no external dependencies needed\n\nThe test confirms:\n- Concurrency is correctly limited to the specified max\n- All cleanup code runs (the \"cleanup ran\" messages show the finally blocks execute)\n- Works with any number of tasks\n\nYou can now use it like:\n```python\nfrom run import run_tasks\n\nasync def my_task():\n    try:\n        # do work\n        pass\n    finally:\n        # cleanup always runs, even on KeyboardInterrupt\n        pass\n\nawait run_tasks([my_task, ...], max_concurrent=4)\n```"
